    "created_at": "2018-12-23T06:16:23Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-12-23T06:16:23Z",
    "body": "> I'd be curious to hear from implementers why they chose these routes differing from the spec.\r\n\r\nBecause the spec requires a _second_ load to happen, no?  That is, it says to create the `<audio>`, set its src... and that's it.  And then the `<audio>` will load the audio data in the \"normal\" way.\r\n\r\nExcept that breaks down completely if the page is a response to a POST request.  And even in the GET case it ends up doing a second, unnecessary, load.\r\n\r\nWhat Gecko does is to create a `<video>` element (for various reasons; I haven't looked into the details), then pumps the incoming data into it.  We do the same thing for `<img>`, actually.  There we do set the `src` attribute, but in a non-spec-possible \"don't actually do the load\" way, then pump data directly into the image from the already-existing response...\r\n\r\nAnyway, the model in the spec is basically not workable for the reasons above.  If we want browsers to converge to the spec, the spec needs to make sense.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
